":-0.014375736728482247,"_gp_share_shrink":true,"_score_share_shrink":68.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.005793553651570788,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":90.3,"_gate_insider_own":0.00155,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":1.0,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Union Pacific Corporation, through its subsidiary, Union Pacific Railroad Company, operates in the railroad business in the United States. It offers transportation services for grain and grain products, fertilizers, food and refrigerated products, and coal and renewables to grain processors, animal feeders, and ethanol and renewable biofuel producers; and construction products, industrial chemicals, plastics, forest products, specialized products, metals and ores, petroleum, liquid petroleum gases, soda ash, and sand, as well as finished automobiles, automotive parts, and merchandise in intermodal containers. The company was founded in 1862 and is headquartered in Omaha, Nebraska.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Vincenzo James Vena | CEO & Director | Age 66 | Compensation: $5.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 28K people. Revenue per employee of $855K ranks in the top quartile among Industrials peers. Each employee generates $192K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 6\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Union Pacific Corporation is a large-cap industrials business with a $170.7B market cap on $24.5B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -0% over the past three years and +1% annually across the full decade \u2014 essentially flat, and a business treading water is rarely a path to compounding.","Returns on capital run at 16% against a 8% cost of capital, a 9-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","The business throws off $5.5B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 22 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.30\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 2.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 7.5\u00d7.","Shareholder yield of 3.5% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 3.6\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Priced near or above estimated fair value, so the margin of safety has been bid away","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.11679015372164421},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 5.7% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Industrials ETF trading well below its long-term trend","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Industrials tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"High market volatility with VIX at 36, signaling elevated fear","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Widening credit spreads suggest growing stress in corporate debt markets","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Broad market in a downtrend, trading below its 200-day average","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Multiple macro indicators signal economic contraction","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely i